{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1PFEEXJzQMGKWQstiJJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up.\n\nThe theorem is true as stated: the solution is `False`, so the proof needs a continuous nonzero `f` on ℝ³ whose integral over every unit sphere vanishes. I found one. Take `f(x) = sin²(π|x|/2)/|x|` (with `f(0) = 0`). For a radial function `g(|x|)`, the sphere integral around a center at distance `r` reduces to `(2π/r)·∫_{|r−1|}^{r+1} s·g(s) ds`. With `s·g(s) = sin²(πs/2)`, this integral is `H(r+1) − H(|r−1|)` where `H(t) = sin²(πt/2)`, and that is zero for all `r ≥ 0` because `H` is 2-periodic and symmetric about 1.\n\nI could not formalize this, for two reasons:\n\n- The file's measure is `μH[2]`, the 2-dimensional Hausdorff measure on the sphere. The Mathlib sources are not available here, and the only sphere-measure file I could find (`MeasureTheory/Constructions/HaarToSphere`) relates to `Measure.toSphere`, not to `μH[2]`. I found no lemma linking `μH[2]` on a sphere to a polar-coordinate or radial integral.\n- Without such a lemma, computing the sphere integral of a radial function is out of reach with the available tools. Making the Bochner integral vanish by non-integrability is not an option either, because a continuous function on a compact sphere with finite Hausdorff measure is integrable.\n\nSo I did not modify `/work/putnam_2019_a4.lean`; it still contains the `sorry`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":395,"cache_read_input_tokens":6601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":395,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da36d14a-b29d-4fa4-a1b9-e8da92140f00","uuid":"6c41083d-1dd5-40ac-82de-d1f5443431d8",